From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Petr=20=C5=A0tetiar?= <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nl80211: cache loaded identities during discovery
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Each reconnect scan resolves every socket in the shared hostapd
directory with GET_CONFIG and STATUS. Three managers repeat this at 500
ms intervals while connecting. Build-18 logs include seven one-second
query timeouts in one boot, including:

  wlan0.1: cmd(GET_CONFIG) timed out
  wlan0.1: cmd(STATUS) timed out

Share a bounded discovery cache keyed by full socket path, device,
inode, ctime and daemon PID/restart/last-failure hints. Reuse only
resolved BSS evidence with a matching ready event client, and rematch it
against current APs on every hit. Keep no AP, manager or process
pointers in the cache. Expire entries after five seconds without
extending their life on a hit, to bound discovery staleness after missed
events or external changes.

Advance a generic connection-layer control epoch before event callbacks,
including TERMINATING, before commands outside an exact read/attachment
allowlist, and on transport failure. Evict only the failing socket path
when GET_CONFIG, STATUS, PING or LINKID 0 PING returns FAIL, an unsupported
command reply or a checked-response mismatch. A previously enabled BSS
must not survive a rejected read in the cache, while zero-link MLD
endpoints must not evict unrelated BSSes on every scan. Keep attachment
and mutating-command failures global. Reject changed inodes and generations. Cache only after a
successful resolution with an unchanged inode/ctime and control epoch.

Emit one WARNING summary when a discovery scan freshly queries more than
three sockets and an individual identity query takes over 200 ms.
Include fresh/cached socket counts and the slowest query in milliseconds
so saved logs can measure cache usefulness during slow scans.

Keep removal snapshots, owner re-resolution and the post-SET identity
check fresh. Leave unresolved sockets and MLD endpoints uncached, so the
zero-link LINKID discriminator and unknown-local/foreign-owner removal
guards retain their existing behaviour. A cold or changing socket still
uses the original synchronous transport and timeout.

Signed-off-by: Petr Štetiar <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
